
# Day 54 – Kubernetes ConfigMaps and Secrets

## 📌 Overview

In Kubernetes, applications often require configuration such as environment variables, database credentials, API keys, and feature flags. Hardcoding these values inside container images leads to poor flexibility and requires rebuilding images for every change.

Kubernetes provides two resources to solve this:

- **ConfigMaps** → for non-sensitive configuration
- **Secrets** → for sensitive data

---

## 🔹 What is a ConfigMap?

A ConfigMap is used to store non-sensitive configuration data in key-value format.

### ✅ Use Cases:
- Application environment variables
- Port numbers
- Feature flags
- Configuration files (e.g., nginx.conf)

### 🔧 Example:
```bash
kubectl create configmap app-config \
  --from-literal=APP_ENV=production \
  --from-literal=APP_DEBUG=false \
  --from-literal=APP_PORT=8080

📌 Key Point:
	•	Stored as plain text
	•	No encryption

⸻

🔹 What is a Secret?

A Secret is used to store sensitive information such as passwords, tokens, and API keys.

✅ Use Cases:
	•	Database credentials
	•	API keys
	•	TLS certificates

🔧 Example:

kubectl create secret generic db-credentials \
  --from-literal=DB_USER=admin \
  --from-literal=DB_PASSWORD=s3cureP@ssw0rd


⸻

⚠️ Important: Base64 is NOT Encryption

Secrets are stored in base64 format:

DB_PASSWORD: czNjdXJlUEBzc3cwcmQ=

Decode:

echo 'czNjdXJlUEBzc3cwcmQ=' | base64 --decode

👉 Output:

s3cureP@ssw0rd

🚨 Conclusion:
	•	Base64 = Encoding, not encryption
	•	Anyone with access can decode it

⸻

🔹 ConfigMap from File

ConfigMaps can also store entire configuration files.

Example: Nginx Config

server {
    listen 80;

    location /health {
        return 200 'healthy';
        add_header Content-Type text/plain;
    }
}

Create:

kubectl create configmap nginx-config \
  --from-file=default.conf=default.conf


⸻

🔹 Using ConfigMaps in Pods

Method 1: Environment Variables

envFrom:
- configMapRef:
    name: app-config

🔍 Behavior:
	•	Injects all key-value pairs as environment variables
	•	Available at container startup

⸻

Method 2: Volume Mount

volumeMounts:
- name: config-volume
  mountPath: /etc/nginx/conf.d

🔍 Behavior:
	•	Mounts ConfigMap as files
	•	Each key becomes a file
	•	Used for large configs (like nginx, apache)

⸻

🔹 Using Secrets in Pods

Inject as Environment Variable

env:
- name: DB_USER
  valueFrom:
    secretKeyRef:
      name: db-credentials
      key: DB_USER


⸻

Mount as Volume

volumes:
- name: secret-volume
  secret:
    secretName: db-credentials

volumeMounts:
- name: secret-volume
  mountPath: /etc/db-credentials
  readOnly: true

🔍 Behavior:
	•	Each key becomes a file
	•	File content is decoded plaintext

⸻

🔥 Environment Variables vs Volume Mounts

Feature	Environment Variables	Volume Mount
Data type	Key-value	Files
Update support	❌ No	✅ Yes
Use case	Simple config	Complex config files


⸻

🔄 ConfigMap Update Behavior

Scenario:
	•	ConfigMap mounted as volume
	•	Value updated using patch command

kubectl patch configmap live-config \
  --type merge \
  -p '{"data":{"message":"world"}}'

Result:
	•	File inside pod updates automatically (30–60 sec)
	•	No pod restart required

⸻

❌ Limitation:
	•	Environment variables do NOT update dynamically
	•	Require pod restart

⸻

🧠 Best Practices
	•	Never store secrets in ConfigMaps
	•	Use Secrets for all sensitive data
	•	Enable encryption at rest in production clusters
	•	Use RBAC to restrict access to Secrets
	•	Prefer volume mounts for dynamic configuration
	•	Avoid hardcoding configuration in container images

⸻

🧪 Verification Commands

kubectl get configmap app-config -o yaml
kubectl get secret db-credentials -o yaml
kubectl exec <pod> -- printenv
kubectl exec <pod> -- cat /etc/db-credentials/DB_PASSWORD


⸻

🧹 Cleanup

kubectl delete pod --all
kubectl delete configmap --all
kubectl delete secret --all


⸻

🚀 Key Takeaways
	•	ConfigMaps and Secrets decouple configuration from code
	•	Secrets are not fully secure without additional measures
	•	Volume-mounted configs update dynamically
	•	Environment variables are static after pod creation
